{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgdZ4t47UjGy9jKJay43r","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Zest says that when it builds a user's personalized map and lists, it uses only the food and drink transactions from the card data it imports through Plaid, a service used by banks, fintech companies and budgeting apps. That history is what the company's new text-message concierge draws on. CEO Mario Gomez-Hall says the concierge answers from your own history and your friends', and that it does not need weeks of conversation to learn you because it starts from where you have actually been.\n\nThe claim is a specific one about the cold-start problem. A general assistant has to ask questions or accumulate chats before it knows whether you favor a neighborhood ramen counter or a long tasting menu, and Zest says it can skip that step because card statements already record the choices. Gomez-Hall says this lets it learn taste faster than a general assistant. The article does not test that comparison. What it does report is TechCrunch's experience: the AI worked fairly well, returning new places matched to its tastes and to details like time of day, party size and location.\n\nThe same data matters in the group chat, where Zest can be added to blend everyone's tastes and settle the choice with a vote. Gomez-Hall also says the advantage is worth something well beyond dinner, and the company is considering recommendations for businesses and shopping. Since Zest describes its data use as limited to food and drink, the article leaves open how that approach would carry over to other categories."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1339,"cache_read_input_tokens":4162,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1339},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","sp